</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(1080px);"><span>Hanson could escape via two strategies:
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(1101.6px);"><span>
</span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(1123.2px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\textbf</span><span>{Institutional Alignment}: Piloting futarchy in municipalities or firms, as suggested in </span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\citep</span><span>{econtalk2007}, though institutional inertia, as Romer faced </span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\citep</span><span>{romer2010}, is a barrier. </span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\\</span><span>
</span></span></div><div style="position: absolute; top: 0px; left: 0px; width: 100%; height: 1.5em; transform: translateY(1144.8px);"><span><span></span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\textbf</span><span>{Polycentric Distribution}: Publishing in open-access formats or policy journals, like Ostrom </span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\citep</span><span>{ostrom1990}, though this risks reduced visibility </span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\citep</span><span>{hanson2009}. Users also navigate this system creatively, such as repurposing the ``like'' button on platforms like YouTube to track viewed content, reflecting hidden motives in engagement </span><span style="color: rgb(198, 120, 221);">\citep</span><span>{hanson2009}. Similarly, Hanson must subvert media incentives to prioritize substance.
