<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">Updated Op-Ed with New Title</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">To align with the essayâ€™s new title and your concern about avoiding "Empire" repetition, Iâ€™ve updated the op-ed to use <em>Clickbait Empire: Robin Hansonâ€™s Struggle Against the Tyranny of Attention</em>. This title maintains the punchy, accessible style while replacing the second "Empire" with "Tyranny of Attention." The content remains ~300 words with inline hyperlinks, incorporating the "like" button comment.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;"><strong class="font-semibold">Clickbait Empire: Robin Hansonâ€™s Struggle Against the Tyranny of Attention</strong></p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Robin Hanson has a bold idea: <a href="https://www.econtalk.org/robin-hanson-on-futarchy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">futarchy</a>, where voters set goalsâ€”like boosting prosperity or cutting inequalityâ€”and prediction markets pick the best policies <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9760.00128" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow"></a>. Itâ€™s practical and testable. So why is he known for <a href="https://grabbyaliens.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">â€œgrabby aliensâ€</a> and sexbots?</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Blame the Clickbait Empire. Podcasts and YouTube crave spectacle. â€œWill aliens rule the galaxy?â€ <a href="https://www.overcomingbias.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow"></a>. Hansonâ€™s trapped, his serious ideas drowned by viral noise.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Compare Hanson to tech pioneers. Guido van Rossum <a href="https://youtu.be/me3sHm11Emg?si=CLOs7hX4oPaFv8O2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow"></a>. Yet, the media casts him as a cosmic showman.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The Clickbait Empire turns thinkers into entertainers. Hanson could pitch futarchy to cities or publish for policy wonks, like Ostrom. But visibility comes at a cost. The real â€œgrabby forceâ€ is the attention economy, capturing ideas and spitting out headlines. We need media that values substance over stargazing.</p>
