<h3 dir="auto" class="text-xl">Addressing the Issue</h3>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">To ensure consistent capitalization:</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words"><strong class="font-semibold">BibTeX Entry Correction</strong>: Update the BibTeX entries to enforce proper capitalization in both <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-200">author</span> and <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-200">title</span> fields. For example, the podcast entry should use <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-200">title = {Robin Hanson and Jordan Stone Talk Galactic X-Risks}</span> with curly braces to preserve case.</li>
<li class="break-words"><strong class="font-semibold">LaTeX Style Consistency</strong>: The <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-200">apalike</span> style typically respects author name capitalization, but some styles (e.g., <span class="text-sm px-1 rounded-sm !font-mono bg-orange-400/10 text-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-300/10 dark:text-orange-200">plain</span>) may alter title case. Using curly braces in titles prevents unwanted lowercase conversion.</li>
<li class="break-words"><strong class="font-semibold">Source Data Verification</strong>: The lowercase â€œhansonâ€ in the podcast title may reflect the original YouTube metadata. Checking the source (e.g., the YouTube video title) and standardizing it manually ensures accuracy.</li>
